  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis is clear and the pathway memberships and connectivity are confirmed. I have everything needed for the narrative.\n\n---\n\nThe differential metabolite list is dominated by eicosanoids and their direct precursors, and the RaMP enrichment confirms this with overwhelming statistical force. **Eicosanoid synthesis** (WikiPathways WP167) is the top hit, with an FDR of 0.0 and a fold-enrichment of 1,111.9 — five of the nine input metabolites map to this pathway (arachidonic acid C00219, leukotriene A4 C00909, 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂ C14717, 5-HETE C04805, and 15-HETE C04742). Secondary to this, multiple SMPDB entries representing NSAID / analgesic action pathways also score as top hits, all sharing the same five matched compounds, which is consistent with drugs that target the arachidonic acid cascade.\n\n**Key drivers.** Arachidonic acid (C00219) is the canonical entry point. KEGG graph traversal confirms it is upstream of leukotriene A4 (C00909) via the two-hop route C00219 → C05356 (5-HPETE) → C00909, placing it as a master precursor. Arachidonic acid is also confirmed as a precursor of 5-HETE (C04805) along the 5-lipoxygenase branch, and of 15-HETE (C04742) along the 15-lipoxygenase branch. Thiosulfate (C05529) and xanthine (C00385) did not match the eicosanoid pathway; they are more plausibly linked to sulfur metabolism and purine catabolism, respectively, and may represent co-incidental changes or independent treatment effects.\n\n**Biological significance.** Coordinated elevation of arachidonic acid together with its downstream eicosanoids — leukotriene A4, 5-HETE, 15-HETE, and the cyclopentenone prostaglandin 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂ — points to activation of both the cyclooxygenase (COX) and lipoxygenase (LOX) arms of the arachidonic acid cascade. Elevated leukotriene A4 and 5-HETE are consistent with pro-inflammatory or immunomodulatory signaling, while 15-HETE and 15-deoxy-PGJ₂ are associated with anti-inflammatory and pro-resolving activity. The androgen precursor 5-androstenediol (C04295) is also present, which may reflect cross-talk between steroid metabolism and inflammatory resolution. Methylmalonic acid (C02170), a propionate catabolite, does not cluster with the eicosanoid module and may indicate a secondary metabolic disturbance. Overall, the profile suggests the treatment strongly engages lipid mediator biosynthesis, skewing the eicosanoid spectrum toward both pro-inflammatory leukotrienes and counter-regulatory prostaglandins.",
